{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgcu289fcDHeiGrLH36Ak","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote the inversion, ran it, and it recovers the signal only partially. The main limit is the method: Tikhonov smoothing softens the sharp edges and leaves ringing.\n\n**Script:** `/private/tmp/e-rhyesG/home/cwd/invert_blur.py`, with the figure saved to `invert_blur.png` in the same directory.\n\n**What it does**\n- Uses your operator and data exactly as you generated them.\n- Checks the adjoint of `Op` with a random dot-product test. It passes.\n- Solves the Tikhonov problem `min ||Op x − y||² + damp²||x||²` with `pylops.optimization.basic.cgls`, matrix-free.\n- Picks the damping by the discrepancy principle. It takes the largest damping whose residual is at or below `σ·√n = 0.02·√200 ≈ 0.283`, using the known noise level.\n- Compares against an oracle damping (chosen with the true signal, for reference only) and an unregularised solve.\n\n**Results (from the run)**\n\n| Estimate | RMSE vs true signal |\n|---|---|\n| Tikhonov, discrepancy-chosen damp = 0.119 | **0.094** (residual 0.275) |\n| Oracle damp = 0.066 | 0.091 |\n| Blurred data `y` used directly | 0.118 |\n| Unregularised (damp = 1e-8) | 713 (residual 0.054; noise amplified wildly) |\n\nThe discrepancy rule lands close to the oracle, so the damping choice is sound. The recovery is still only a modest improvement over the blurred data. The plot shows the two plateaus and the negative block are located correctly, but the edges are smeared over several samples. There is also ringing around the step at 40 and 150.\n\n**Choices I made that you may want to change**\n- Used Tikhonov (L2) regularisation. It is simple but favours smooth solutions, which is why the edges blur.\n- Used the discrepancy principle because the noise level is known. Without a known σ, I would use an L-curve or GCV instead.\n\n**Suggested next step:** the true signal is piecewise constant, so a sparsity-promoting penalty should sharpen the edges considerably. Options are total variation (gradient-sparse) or L1 on a suitable transform, solved with pylops' FISTA or split-Bregman solvers. I have not implemented or run that. Say if you want it added."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1785,"cache_read_input_tokens":22694,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1785},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"98b2cddd-5be1-4fc4-b3c2-c8c39098181e","uuid":"0a61ef1d-06b9-4500-bc60-76d045c1b97d","timestamp":"2026-11-01T21:12:55.244Z","request_id":"req_011Cgcu27wmq2Zp6XR65oQc1"}
